  "worked_solution": "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-16{y}^{2}-54x+64y & =-1 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-54x-16{y}^{2}+64y & =-1 \\\\ 9[{x}^{2}-6x]-16[{y}^{2}-4y] & =-1 \\\\ 9[(x-3)^{2}-9]-16[(y-2)^{2}-4] & =-1 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2}-81-16(y-2)^{2}+64 & =-1 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2}-16(y-2)^{2} & =-1+81-64 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2}-16(y-2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,3,2\\,).$",
